course: everyday
lesson: 335
title: Fake websites, fake news and bots, skipping a lesson, keeping surprises and being trusted
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tricksters build fake websites that look almost exactly like real ones, with a tiny change in the address, like «amaz0n» with a zero instead of «amazon». So you read the address carefully, letter by letter.
explain: A shocking headline in big capital letters is written to make you click, and it can mislead. Read beyond the headline and check other sources. Some sites, like The Onion, write satire: made-up news as a joke. And some accounts are bots or fake accounts that post the same message again and again to make an idea look more popular than it is.
explain: If a friend pressures you to skip a lesson, stay in class: skipping gets you in trouble and worries the school staff, who must always know where you are.
explain: Some secrets are happy ones: if you find out about a Christmas present, you keep quiet and act surprised, so the giver keeps their joy. Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild: after a lie, telling the truth again and again slowly wins it back, and behaving well when the teacher is out of the room is what makes you trustworthy.
fact: Fake websites copy real ones with tiny spelling changes in the address, like «amaz0n» instead of «amazon».
fact: Check a website's address carefully before typing anything into it.
fact: Headlines are written to make you click and can be misleading, so read beyond the headline.
fact: Checking whether other trusted sources report the same story helps you spot fake news.
fact: Satire is made-up news written as a joke; it is not lying when readers know it is a joke, but it can fool people who don't.
fact: Some accounts are bots or fake accounts that repeat the same message to make an idea look more popular than it is.
fact: If a friend pressures you to skip a lesson, stay in class; skipping gets you in trouble and worries school staff, who must know where you are.
fact: If you find out about a surprise present, keep quiet and act surprised; spoiling it takes the joy from the giver.
fact: Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild.
fact: After a lie, keep telling the truth, and trust will grow back slowly.
fact: Being good when no one is watching is what makes you trustworthy.
exercise: A boy gets a link to «www.amaz0n-deals.com». What should he notice? | The address has a zero instead of an «o», so it is a fake site | It is the real site, because it has the word «amazon» right there in the address | Every shop uses zeros in its address | The link must be real because it came by message
exercise: Why do fake websites look almost exactly like real ones? | They copy real ones to trick people into trusting them | The real shop lends them its pages to sell more things | All websites use the same pictures | It is just a coincidence
exercise: What should you check before typing a password into a website? | That the address is spelled exactly right | That the website has bright, friendly colours | That the page loads quickly | That it shows a lot of adverts
exercise: You see the headline «SCHOOLS TO BAN HOMEWORK FOREVER!!!» What is the wise first step? | Read beyond the headline and check other sources | Believe it, since capital letters mean it is important | Share it at once with your class | Stop doing homework from tomorrow
exercise: Why are some headlines written in big shocking words? | To make you click, even if the story says less | Because capital letters mean the facts have been checked | Because the news is always very important | So that they are easier to spell
exercise: A girl reads a story on The Onion saying a cat was elected mayor of a big city. What is The Onion? | A satire site that writes made-up news as jokes | A serious newspaper that checks every fact twice before printing | A website about cooking onions | A government news office
exercise: Which is TRUE about satire? | It is not lying when readers know it is a joke, but it can fool people who don't | It is real news, just written in a funny voice so that children will read it too | It is always a dangerous lie | Nobody is ever fooled by satire
exercise: Fifty accounts with no photos post the very same message about a new game. What might this be? | Bots or fake accounts making the idea look more popular | Fifty real fans who all happened to write the same words | A proof that the game is the best ever | A message from the game's real players' club
exercise: Why do people use bots to repeat a message many times? | To make an idea look more popular than it is | To save the internet from slowing down when lots of people post | To help the message be spelled better | To make sure nobody reads it
exercise: Your friend says, «Let's skip maths and hide in the toilets». What should you do? | Stay in class | Go with him, but only for half the lesson | Skip, but tell nobody | Go if the lesson is boring
exercise: Why does skipping a lesson worry school staff? | They must always know where every child is | Teachers get fined for each empty desk in their room | They want to count the chairs | It makes the classroom too quiet
exercise: You see your birthday present hidden in your parents' wardrobe. What is the kind thing to do? | Keep quiet and act surprised on the day | Tell them you found it, so they can hide it better | Open it early and wrap it back up again neatly | Tell your brother so that he knows about it too
exercise: Why should you keep quiet about a surprise present you found? | Spoiling it takes the joy from the giver | Presents disappear if you talk about them | Parents only ever buy presents in great secret | You might get a second present if you ask nicely
exercise: A girl lied about her homework, and now her parents check her bag every day. How can she win back their trust? | Keep telling the truth, and trust will grow back slowly | Say sorry once and then demand that they stop checking her bag | Hide her bag so they cannot check | Lie better so they do not find out
exercise: Which is TRUE about trust? | It is quick to lose and slow to rebuild | It comes back the moment you say sorry and mean it | Once lost, it can never ever come back again | It is easy to build and very hard to lose
exercise: The teacher leaves the classroom for five minutes. What does a trustworthy pupil do? | Behave as if the teacher were still there | Do whatever she likes until the door opens | Read other pupils' work on the teacher's desk | Go into the corridor to play
test: An email says «Your PayPaI account is locked» with a capital «I» instead of the «l». What does this tiny change suggest? | It is likely a fake message copying a real company | It is a sign the email is extra safe and has been checked | It is a normal spelling for that company | The company changes its name each month
test: A post says, «Scientists say chocolate makes you fly!» Only one strange site has the story. Which is the best check? | See whether trusted news sources report it too | Share it to ask your friends whether it is true or not | Count how many likes it has | Believe it if the picture looks real
test: A grandmother reads a satire story about the Queen's corgis becoming judges and thinks it is real. Why could this happen? | Satire can fool people who don't know it is a joke | Satire is always marked in big red letters at the top | The story must have been true | Satire sites never write about animals
test: Hundreds of new accounts, all made on the same day, keep posting «Everyone hates the new park!». Which is TRUE? | They may be bots, so the idea may be less popular than it looks | Hundreds of posts on the same day prove that nearly everyone in the town hates it | Bots only ever post true things | It shows the park will be closed tomorrow
test: An older boy dares a girl in Brisbane to slip out of class without asking. Why should she stay? | Leaving gets her in trouble and staff must know where she is | It is fine if she comes back before the bell, because nobody gets hurt | Brave pupils always say yes to dares | The teacher will not notice one missing pupil
test: A boy hears his family planning a surprise party for his dad. At dinner, his dad asks, «Anything happening this weekend?» What is the kind answer? | Keep the surprise and talk about something else | Say «I can't say, it's a secret party for you!» | Tell him all about the party | Laugh and point at the cupboard with the cake
test: A boy told one lie last month. Why might his friends still double-check what he says? | Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild | Friends never forgive even a single small lie | He has told the truth far too often since then | His friends cannot remember the lie any more
test: A test is left on the desk while the teacher steps out. Nobody would ever know if you looked at the answers. What shows you are trustworthy? | Leaving it alone, as if the teacher were there | Looking quickly and putting it back exactly | Looking only at the hardest question | Asking a friend to look for you
explain: A man in Ireland typed his bank details into a website with one letter wrong in the address; the money in his account was taken within hours.
explain: A boy in Texas skipped a lesson because a friend pushed him to; nobody knew where he was, the school called his mum, and she left work frightened.
fact: Fake websites steal passwords and money every day, often using an address only one letter different from the real one.
fact: When a child skips a lesson, the school must treat it as a missing child until they know the child is safe.
test: amaz0n dot com wants my login | Don't; the address is fake, tell a parent | Type it in, it looks right to me | Type in an old password | Type it fast before it closes
test: account posting the same news 100 times, must be true | It may be a bot; check trusted sources | Yes, if many repeat it | Yes, if it's angry | Yes, if it has a blue tick
test: mate says skip science, no one'll notice | Stay in class; school will think you're missing | Skip if you come back before the bell goes | Skip it, he's right | Skip and hide in the library
test: did u hear a kid typed his password into "amaz0n" with a zero and "they took 2 grand off his dad's card". lesson? | Check a website's address letter by letter before typing anything | Only type passwords on your phone, fake sites don't work on phones | Sites with zeros are always safe | Fake sites can't take real money
test: my mate says a headline said "school holidays cancelled forever" so it's true. what's likely | Headlines mislead; read beyond them and check other trusted sources | If it's online in big letters it's real, they can't just make that up | Headlines are always true | Holidays are being cancelled
test: kids say the boy who skipped class "ran away from home and lives in the woods now". pass it on? | No, it's a made-up rumour; don't spread it | Yes, it's exciting news | Yes, and add more details | Yes, warn everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method by which fraudulent websites deceive their visitors? | They use addresses differing by a single letter from genuine ones | They always display obvious warnings at the top of every page | They are always slow to load | They use only foreign languages
exam: Identify the principal means of detecting fake news. | Checking whether other trusted sources report the same story | Counting how many people have shared it, liked it or commented upon it | Reading only the headline | Trusting whichever story is most exciting
exam: It is given that a friend presses a pupil to skip a lesson. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The pupil remains in class, since the school must know where pupils are | The pupil skips the lesson if it is a short one and returns before it ends | The pupil skips if the friend insists | The pupil hides until lunch
exam: State how a school must treat a pupil who has skipped a lesson until their whereabouts are known. | As a missing child | As a pupil on a break | As a pupil who has gone home | As a matter of no concern
